    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d182">

                <pb facs="0374" n="248" xml:id="pg248"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">121.5762/3</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-08-20"><hi rendition="#italic">August 20,
                        1925</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received September 3.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 198</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to report that, in
                    compliance with the Department’s instruction No. 3821 of April 21, 1925,<anchor
                        n="77" xml:id="d182-f1"/> I informed the Foreign Office that certain agents
                    of the Customs Division of the United States Treasury had been attached to the
                    Embassy and requested recognition of them in their respective capacities by the
                    German Government.</p>
                <p>The Embassy is now in receipt of a Foreign Office Note Verbale, No. Ill A 1977,
                    of the seventeenth instant in reply, a copy and translation of which are
                    transmitted herewith,<anchor n="78" xml:id="d182-f2"/> in which the Foreign
                    Office states that the German Government is not in a position to accord the
                    recognition requested, since, in the opinion of the Foreign Office, the duties
                    which the “contemplated American officials” are to perform in Germany are not
                    duties which should, according to international regulations or international
                    usage, be performed by officials of a diplomatic career. The Foreign Office adds
                    that for this reason, the German Government has misgivings of a fundamental
                    nature against having officials who have to perform the contemplated duties
                    attached to a diplomatic mission in Berlin.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Jacob Gould
                    Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
